16:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 7–8, 1864","place":"Wilderness battlefield, Brock Road, Todd’s Tavern, Laurel Hill, and Spotsylvania Court House, Virginia","result":"Grant moved southeast instead of retreating after the Wilderness, but Lee anticipated the move. Anderson’s Confederate infantry marched through the night, reinforced Stuart’s cavalry at Laurel Hill, and stopped Warren’s V Corps short of the Spotsylvania crossroads. Grant then allowed Sheridan to leave with most of the U.S. cavalry to fight Stuart","facts":[{"label":"U.S. force after the Wilderness","value":"About 102,000"},{"label":"U.S. Wilderness casualties","value":"More than 17,500"},{"label":"Confederate force engaged in the Wilderness","value":"Just over 61,000"},{"label":"Confederate Wilderness casualties","value":"About 13,000"},{"label":"Wilderness to Spotsylvania","value":"About 10 miles"},{"label":"Distance across Laurel Hill field","value":"About 400 yards"},{"label":"Range of the decisive volleys","value":"About 60 yards"},{"label":"Sheridan’s resupply destination","value":"Haxall’s Landing, more than 60 miles away"}],"maps":[{"title":"The race to Laurel Hill, 3–10 a.m.","caption":"The first National Park Service map shows Warren moving south on Brock Road, Fitzhugh Lee’s cavalry delaying him, Anderson’s infantry arriving from the west, and Wilson’s cavalry reaching Spotsylvania Court House farther south.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=23794f35-daf4-4cee-b24e-d58dc3e6b22e","image":"/images/spotsylvania-may8-morning-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed map of troop movements toward Laurel Hill and Spotsylvania Court House from 3 to 10 a.m. on May 8, 1864"},{"title":"The fighting at Laurel Hill, 10 a.m.–6 p.m.","caption":"The second map shows V Corps attacks along Brock Road, Kershaw’s Confederate line around Laurel Hill, additional Confederate infantry arriving, and the U.S. line extending east during the afternoon.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=f1f62d5a-edd4-41c9-a96f-13c476ef010c","image":"/images/spotsylvania-may8-afternoon-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed map of troop positions and attacks at Laurel Hill from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. on May 8, 1864"},{"title":"The Wilderness–Spotsylvania corridor today","caption":"Brock Road runs southeast from the Wilderness through Todd’s Tavern to Laurel Hill. Confederate columns used parallel roads to reach the courthouse from the west and south.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.88%2C38.17%2C-77.50%2C38.40&layer=mapnik&marker=38.2167%2C-77.6000"},{"title":"The movement within the Overland Campaign","caption":"The campaign map shows Grant’s turn southeast from the Wilderness to Spotsylvania rather than north across the Rapidan. Lee’s army remained between Grant and Richmond.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign between the Wilderness and Spotsylvania"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:18","audioTime":78,"title":"The Wilderness battle ended without a decision","detail":"The episode counts more than 17,500 U.S. casualties from about 102,000 engaged and about 13,000 Confederate casualties from just over 61,000."},{"time":"2:12","audioTime":132,"title":"Grant decided not to retreat","detail":"He broke contact in the Wilderness and moved southeast around Lee’s right toward Spotsylvania Court House."},{"time":"3:50","audioTime":230,"title":"Both armies remained behind their works on May 7","detail":"Neither renewed the main battle. Patrols and skirmishers remained active while commanders determined the next movement."},{"time":"4:28","audioTime":268,"title":"Grant shifted his supply line east","detail":"Pontoon bridges were removed from Germanna Ford and sent to Ely’s Ford to speed evacuation of wounded soldiers toward Fredericksburg."},{"time":"4:56","audioTime":296,"title":"Lee concluded Grant was moving away from the Rapidan","detail":"The abandoned Germanna Ford route showed that Grant was not retreating north. Lee identified Fredericksburg and Spotsylvania as the two likely destinations."},{"time":"5:34","audioTime":334,"title":"Roads made Spotsylvania strategically important","detail":"Brock Road met routes from Fredericksburg and Richmond near the courthouse. A U.S. arrival first would place Grant between Lee and Richmond."},{"time":"6:29","audioTime":389,"title":"Lee sent Stuart to cover the roads","detail":"At about 2 p.m. on May 7, Confederate cavalry received orders to delay any U.S. movement toward Spotsylvania."},{"time":"7:06","audioTime":426,"title":"Anderson’s corps received the lead march assignment","detail":"James Longstreet was wounded, so Richard Anderson led the corps nearest Spotsylvania. Its position lacked a direct road south."},{"time":"8:17","audioTime":497,"title":"Confederates opened a military road","detail":"William Pendleton cut a rough track from Anderson’s position to Catharpin Road. Infantry could use it, but the artillery had to take the longer route through Parker’s Store."},{"time":"8:52","audioTime":532,"title":"Lee ordered Anderson to move at 3 a.m.","detail":"The original plan was to leave the works after dark, rest briefly, and begin the main march early on May 8."},{"time":"9:33","audioTime":573,"title":"Anderson continued through the night","detail":"His troops started by 10 p.m. Fires and rough ground prevented a bivouac, so the corps kept marching toward Spotsylvania without rest."},{"time":"10:26","audioTime":626,"title":"The Army of the Potomac began its night march","detail":"Warren moved down Brock Road, Sedgwick took a route through Chancellorsville, and Burnside and Hancock covered the rear."},{"time":"10:45","audioTime":645,"title":"Traffic stalled the U.S. columns","detail":"Ambulances, artillery, infantry, fatigue, and darkness backed up Brock Road for miles and slowed the march to a few steps at a time."},{"time":"12:21","audioTime":741,"title":"Meade found two cavalry divisions at Todd’s Tavern","detail":"Gregg and Merritt had no instructions about the night movement even though Meade expected Sheridan’s cavalry to screen the route."},{"time":"15:29","audioTime":929,"title":"Meade issued orders directly to Gregg and Merritt","detail":"Gregg was sent toward Corbin’s Bridge and Merritt toward Blockhouse Bridge through Spotsylvania to deny the Po River crossings to Lee."},{"time":"18:29","audioTime":1109,"title":"The cavalry left Todd’s Tavern at 3 a.m.","detail":"Gregg moved west on Catharpin Road while Merritt rode south on Brock Road."},{"time":"18:53","audioTime":1133,"title":"Fitzhugh Lee blocked Brock Road","detail":"Felled trees, dismounted cavalry, and artillery stopped Merritt about two miles south of Todd’s Tavern. By daylight his entire division was engaged."},{"time":"19:37","audioTime":1177,"title":"Merritt requested infantry support","detail":"John C. Robinson’s V Corps division was about a mile behind. Warren sent Robinson’s leading brigade to clear the road."},{"time":"20:36","audioTime":1236,"title":"Sheridan deployed the arriving infantry","detail":"Robinson’s troops initially pushed the Confederate cavalry south, but heat and exhaustion quickly slowed them."},{"time":"21:27","audioTime":1287,"title":"Confederates formed a position at Laurel Hill","detail":"Fitzhugh Lee’s troopers built abatis and fence-rail breastworks along a pine-covered rise with a clear field of fire across roughly 400 yards."},{"time":"22:27","audioTime":1347,"title":"Warren ordered an immediate attack","detail":"Robinson preferred to wait for his full division, but Warren decided to attack before the defense could grow stronger."},{"time":"23:05","audioTime":1385,"title":"Peter Lyle’s brigade reached within 60 yards","detail":"A close Confederate volley stopped the advance. V Corps brigades then entered the fight separately as each arrived."},{"time":"25:09","audioTime":1509,"title":"Robinson was severely wounded","detail":"A bullet shattered his left knee. The leg was amputated, ending his field service, and the V Corps assault collapsed."},{"time":"26:12","audioTime":1572,"title":"Anderson’s infantry reached Laurel Hill","detail":"Two brigades from Joseph Kershaw’s division reinforced the cavalry in time to repel Warren’s attacks. Two others continued to secure Spotsylvania Court House."},{"time":"27:06","audioTime":1626,"title":"Wilson reached the courthouse and withdrew","detail":"James Wilson’s cavalry arrived before Kershaw’s infantry, but Sheridan ordered it back because it was isolated and unsupported."},{"time":"27:35","audioTime":1655,"title":"Meade learned Confederate infantry held the road","detail":"He ordered Sedgwick to deploy VI Corps on Warren’s left and directed both corps to attack again."},{"time":"28:55","audioTime":1735,"title":"Meade confronted Sheridan","detail":"Meade blamed the cavalry for failing to screen the march and block Confederate routes. Sheridan objected to Meade issuing orders directly to cavalry division commanders."},{"time":"30:17","audioTime":1817,"title":"Grant authorized Sheridan’s independent raid","detail":"When told Sheridan said he could defeat Stuart, Grant approved the proposal. Meade ordered the cavalry corps to move against Confederate cavalry and later resupply at Haxall’s Landing."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant and George G. Meade","command":"Overall command","subordinates":"Directed the movement from the Wilderness and the effort to seize Spotsylvania"},{"commander":"Gouverneur K. Warren","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"John C. Robinson and Charles Griffin’s divisions attacked at Laurel Hill"},{"commander":"John Sedgwick","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Moved through Chancellorsville and was ordered to reinforce Warren"},{"commander":"Winfield Scott Hancock and Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"II and IX Corps","subordinates":"Covered the army’s withdrawal from the Wilderness"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"David Gregg, Wesley Merritt, and James H. Wilson’s divisions"},{"commander":"Wesley Merritt","command":"1st Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Fought Fitzhugh Lee on Brock Road and called for V Corps infantry support"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Anticipated the Spotsylvania movement and ordered cavalry and infantry south"},{"commander":"Richard H. Anderson","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Joseph Kershaw and Charles Field’s divisions marched through the night"},{"commander":"Joseph B. Kershaw","command":"Infantry division","subordinates":"Two brigades reinforced Laurel Hill while two secured the courthouse"},{"commander":"J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Directed the delaying defense at Laurel Hill"},{"commander":"Fitzhugh Lee","command":"Cavalry division","subordinates":"Blocked Brock Road with felled trees, dismounted troopers, artillery, and fieldworks"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Richard H. Anderson","role":"Led Longstreet’s corps through the night and reached Spotsylvania before Warren","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Placed cavalry on the roads and directed Kershaw’s arriving infantry at Laurel Hill","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/jeb-stuart.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671039/"},{"name":"Fitzhugh Lee","role":"Conducted the mounted and dismounted delaying action along Brock Road","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Gouverneur K. Warren","role":"Pushed V Corps down Brock Road and committed brigades separately against Laurel Hill","side":"United States"},{"name":"John C. Robinson","role":"Led V Corps infantry at Laurel Hill until a leg wound ended his field service","side":"United States"},{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Clashed with Meade over the cavalry’s role and received permission to leave on a raid against Stuart","side":"United States","image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666507/"},{"name":"Wesley Merritt","role":"Led the cavalry advance down Brock Road and fought Fitzhugh Lee’s delaying force","side":"United States"},{"name":"James H. Wilson","role":"Reached Spotsylvania Court House first but withdrew on Sheridan’s order because his division was unsupported","side":"United States"}],"losses":[{"name":"John C. Robinson","side":"United States","role":"Brigadier general commanding a V Corps division","outcome":"Shot through the left knee while leading the Laurel Hill attack; his leg was amputated and he did not return to field command"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/spotsylvania-may8-morning-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed NPS map of the early May 8 race to Laurel Hill","caption":"The morning map shows the cavalry delaying action, Warren’s approach, Anderson’s night march, and Wilson’s isolated arrival at the courthouse.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=23794f35-daf4-4cee-b24e-d58dc3e6b22e"},{"image":"/images/spotsylvania-may8-afternoon-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed NPS map of the May 8 afternoon battle at Laurel Hill","caption":"By afternoon, Confederate infantry held the Laurel Hill line while Warren extended V Corps east and waited for Sedgwick.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=f1f62d5a-edd4-41c9-a96f-13c476ef010c"},{"image":"/images/jeb-stuart.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Confederate cavalry commander J. E. B. Stuart","caption":"Stuart’s cavalry delayed the U.S. advance long enough for Anderson’s infantry to occupy Laurel Hill.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671039/"},{"image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","alt":"Portrait of U.S. cavalry commander Philip H. Sheridan","caption":"Sheridan’s dispute with Meade ended with the cavalry corps leaving the main army to seek a battle with Stuart.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666507/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 484","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/02/09/civil-war-podcast-episode-484/"},{"label":"National Park Service: history of Spotsylvania Court House","url":"https://www.nps.gov/frsp/learn/historyculture/spotsylvania.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Spotsylvania battlefield exhibit shelter","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/spotsylvania-battlefield-exhibit-shelter.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: May 8 troop movements, 3–10 a.m.","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=23794f35-daf4-4cee-b24e-d58dc3e6b22e"},{"label":"National Park Service: May 8 troop movements, 10 a.m.–6 p.m.","url":"https://www.nps.gov/media/photo/gallery-item.htm?gid=3B20E227-7F2C-41EB-92EF-56C1914D6A42&id=f1f62d5a-edd4-41c9-a96f-13c476ef010c"},{"label":"Library of Congress: J. E. B. Stuart portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671039/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Philip Sheridan portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666507/"}]}}]}]
